<div class="help-prose">
    <div class="help-callout"><strong>Your information has a public-safety purpose.</strong><p>Share only what is needed for your account, report, or inquiry. Never include your password or verification code in a support message.</p></div>
    <section><h4>1. Information involved</h4><p>Depending on the feature you use, PyroSense processes account and contact details, assigned roles, verification records, report descriptions, submitted coordinates, attachments, and incident updates. Security records can include IP addresses, device/browser information, timestamps, and account activity.</p></section>
    <section><h4>2. Why information is used</h4><p>These records support account verification and recovery, incident assessment, response coordination, prevention analysis, and investigation of misuse. The information needed for an emergency report differs from the information needed for an optional support inquiry.</p></section>
    <section><h4>3. Location and anonymous reporting</h4><p>Your browser asks permission before providing device location. Check the proposed position before reporting. Reporting without a registered account does not mean that a submission is untraceable: coordinates, attachments, and technical security records may still identify a person or place.</p></section>
    <section><h4>4. Access and service providers</h4><p>Information is used by the system and authorized personnel involved in the relevant workflow. Hosting, email, mapping, communication, or analysis services may process information required for an enabled feature. External websites have their own privacy notices. Ask the operator for the current provider list and the handling of a particular record.</p></section>
    <section><h4>5. Device storage and retention</h4><p>The installed app or browser may store session information, saved pages, preferences, map data, and pending reports locally. Be careful on shared devices and sign out when finished. A fixed record-retention schedule is not published here; ask the operator how long the specific record is kept. Deletion requests may be subject to applicable recordkeeping obligations.</p></section>
    <section><h4>6. Your privacy rights</h4><p>Under applicable Philippine privacy law, you may have rights to information, access, correction, objection, erasure or blocking, and data portability, subject to legal conditions. You may also raise a complaint with the National Privacy Commission. See the <a href="https://privacy.gov.ph/data-subject-rights/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NPC guide to data-subject rights</a>.</p></section>
    <section><h4>7. How to raise a concern</h4><p>Ask the station administering your account for the responsible project operator or privacy contact. State the record or concern without sending unnecessary personal documents. The operator may need to verify your identity before releasing or changing protected information.</p></section>
    <section><h4>8. Notice and consent</h4><p>Reading this notice is not permission for unrelated uses of your information. The responsible operator must explain the applicable basis for processing, including any additional optional use, and communicate material changes to this notice.</p></section>
</div>
